[2026-11-13 04:07:05] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment

Reviewing all experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured (non-null):

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-0add-5deg-550s-N2-1450s | 912.84 | 0.27 | Top gap (coverage defect) |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.49 | 0.27 | Wrinkle |
| 19-0add-5deg-0s-N2-1500s (run4) | 890.19 | 0.29 | Minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s (run2) | 960.52 | 0.27 | Clean |
| 17p5-0add-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.59 | 0.27 | Minor undulations |
| 17p5-0add-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run6) | 699.80 | 0.26 | Clean |
| 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 385.22 | 0.41 | Minor wrinkles |
| 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run5) | 468.62 | 0.39 | Wrinkles |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run4) | 330.99 | 0.44 | Clean! |
| 19-0add-5deg-0s-N2-1500s (run6) | 468.93 | 0.39 | Minor top gap |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-200s-NoN2-1800s | 340.70 | 0.38 | Clean |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.47 | 0.40 | Minor fold |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-100s-NoN2-1800s | 520.58 | 0.37 | Wrinkle |

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~960 bar / 0.27 (run2 of 17p5-20deg-90s)
- Balanced: ~700 bar / 0.30–0.33 (several candidates)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~330–400 bar / 0.39–0.44

The **high-pore-fraction end** is particularly interesting: `17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s_run4` achieved Pore Fraction = 0.44 with Modulus = 331 bar (clean membrane, good image quality). This is the highest pore fraction observed with a clean membrane. However, it is based on a single specimen with only 1 retained fit (SD = 0), making it uncertain.

The **high-modulus end**: `17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s_run2` achieved 960 bar / 0.27 but with very high SD (387 bar). Run3 of the same condition gave only 385 bar / 0.33. These two runs are highly inconsistent — the condition is not validated.

### Validation Policy Check

**`17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s_run4`** (bath_temp=19, polymer_wt=17.5, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=90, nips_bath_wait_time=1800, mixing_temp=65, pullcast_speed=10, N2=true):
- Run1: 860 bar / 0.27 (wrinkle)
- Run2: 554 bar / 0.29 (wrinkles)
- Run3: 519 bar / 0.36 (wrinkles)
- Run4: 331 bar / 0.44 (clean!) — single retained fit, SD=0
- Run5: 552 bar / 0.32 (coverage gap + wrinkle)
- Run6: 64 bar / 0.44 (severely misloaded — invalid)

The pore fraction = 0.44 from run4 is a single-fit result with SD=0, and run6 also shows 0.44 but is clearly a failed/misloaded specimen. The modulus of 331 bar from run4 is dramatically lower than other runs. This is likely a measurement artifact from a poorly loaded or unusual specimen rather than a true Pareto improvement. The condition shows extreme variability across runs.

**`17p5-0add-20deg-90s-N2-1250s`** (bath_temp=20, polymer_wt=17.5, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=90, nips_bath_wait_time=1250, mixing_temp=50, pullcast_speed=7, N2=true):
- Run1: 673 bar / 0.29 (clean)
- Run2: 961 bar / 0.27 (clean, but SD=387 bar — very high)
- Run3: 385 bar / 0.33 (clean)
- Run5: 530 bar / 0.35 (wrinkle)

This condition has 4 runs with high variability. Not validated as a reliable high-modulus candidate.

### Strategy

Given the high variability across the dataset and the lack of confirmed Pareto-front candidates, the most informative next step is to **explore a region that could genuinely improve both objectives simultaneously** — specifically, conditions that might yield moderate-to-high modulus (>600 bar) AND moderate-to-high pore fraction (>0.33).

Looking at the data pattern:
- **Lower bath temperatures** (5°C) with **higher polymer concentrations** (19–20 wt%) tend to give higher modulus but lower pore fraction
- **Higher bath temperatures** (15–20°C) with **lower polymer concentrations** (17–17.5 wt%) and **longer wait times** or **no N2** tend to give higher pore fraction but lower modulus
- The **18 wt% / bath 15°C / NoN2 / long wait** region (18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s) shows pore fractions of 0.36–0.41 with moduli of 340–470 bar — interesting but modulus is low

An **under-explored region** that could potentially combine moderate-high modulus with higher pore fraction: **polymer_wt ~18–18.5, bath_temp ~10–12°C, moderate wait time ~75–90s, N2=true, mixing_temp=65**. The 18-0add-12deg-90s-N2-1800s condition showed 581 bar / 0.28 (run1, clean) — decent modulus but low pore fraction. 

A genuinely under-sampled region: **polymer_wt ~18, bath_temp ~20°C, moderate wait, N2=true**. Bath temp 20°C with 18 wt% has not been tried. Higher bath temp tends to increase pore fraction; 18 wt% should maintain reasonable modulus vs 17 wt%.

Another gap: **pullcast_speed** has been almost exclusively at 10 mm/s in recent experiments. Lower pullcast speed (e.g., 5–6 mm/s) with a promising condition could reduce wrinkling and improve membrane quality.

**Recommendation**: Try **polymer_wt=18.0, bath_temp=20°C, mixing_temp=65, pullcast_speed=6, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=75, nips_bath_wait_time=1800, N2=true**. 

Rationale:
- bath_temp=20°C is under-sampled at 18 wt% (only 17.5 wt% has been tried at bath_temp=20)
- Higher bath temp should promote higher pore fraction vs bath_temp=10–15°C
- 18 wt% polymer should maintain better modulus than 17–17.5 wt%
- pullcast_speed=6 (vs the dominant 10) may reduce wrinkling tendency
- This is a genuinely new point in the design space

