[2026-11-13 06:53:12] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment

Let me identify the non-dominated candidates from experiments with **both** Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17p5-550s-5deg | 912.84 | 0.27 | Top gap defect |
| 19-0s-5deg (run1) | 767.83 | 0.34 | Good |
| 20-150s-5deg (run1) | 755.94 | 0.34 | Good |
| 17p5-19deg-90s (run1) | 860.49 | 0.27 | Wrinkle |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 (run3) | 385.22 | 0.41 | Minor wrinkle |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-run4 | 330.99 | 0.44 | Good quality! |
| 19-0s-5deg-run4 | 890.19 | 0.29 | Minor wrinkles |
| 19-0s-5deg-run3 | 614.09 | 0.32 | Wrinkles |

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~860–912 bar / 0.27
- Balanced: ~767 bar / 0.34 (19wt%, 5°C bath, 0s wait)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~330–385 bar / 0.41–0.44

The **19wt%, 5°C bath, 0s wait, N2, 1500s** condition (run1: 767 bar / 0.34) is a strong balanced Pareto candidate. It has 3 independent repeats with modulus measurements (run1, run3, run4). Run1 and run3 are reasonably consistent (~614–768 bar, 0.32–0.34 PF), but run4 shows a large jump to 890 bar / 0.29 with high CV (0.22) and high SD (250 bar). This variability warrants caution.

**The 17p5-19deg-90s-run4** (330 bar / 0.44) is intriguing for high pore fraction but has only 1 specimen with a single retained fit (SD=0), making it unreliable.

### Validation vs. Exploration Decision

The **19wt%, 5°C, 0s wait, N2, 1500s** condition has 3 independent synthesis repeats with modulus data (runs 1, 3, 4), showing:
- Run 1: 767 bar / 0.34 PF
- Run 3: 614 bar / 0.32 PF  
- Run 4: 890 bar / 0.29 PF (high SD, high CV, wrinkles)

The spread is large (614–890 bar) and run 4 has quality concerns. This condition is partially validated but uncertain. However, the policy says "once performance is reproducible, resume searching instead of repeating indefinitely." Three runs exist; the mean is roughly 757 bar / 0.32 PF — a solid balanced point. Further repeats here have diminishing returns.

### Exploration Target

The data shows a clear pattern: **lower bath temperature (5°C) with moderate-to-high polymer concentration (19–20 wt%) and N2** tends to give higher modulus. **Higher bath temperature (15–19°C) with lower polymer (17–17.5 wt%) and no-N2 with longer wait** tends to give higher pore fraction.

An **under-explored region** that could push the Pareto frontier: **moderate polymer (18.5–19 wt%), cold bath (5°C), N2, short wait (~30–60s), moderate pullcast speed (~5–8 mm/s)**. This combines the modulus-favoring cold bath with a polymer concentration that might preserve more pore fraction than 20 wt%.

Specifically, **18.5 wt%, 5°C bath, 60s wait, N2, mixing_temp 60°C, pullcast 5 mm/s, 1600s NIPS** is not in the history. This is distinct from the 18.5wt% experiments which used bath temps of 5°C with 105s wait (runs 1&2, both had defects/bubbles) or 7°C/12°C/14°C. A 5°C bath with 60s wait and slower pullcast speed (5 mm/s vs 11 mm/s) could reduce wrinkling while maintaining the cold-bath modulus advantage.

**Expected benefit**: Cold bath (5°C) should favor higher modulus via faster phase separation; 18.5 wt% is intermediate between the high-modulus 19–20 wt% and the high-pore-fraction 17–17.5 wt% region; 60s wait allows slight surface skinning without excessive evaporation; slower pullcast (5 mm/s) may reduce wrinkling seen at 11 mm/s for this polymer weight. This could yield a Pareto improvement over the 19wt%/5°C point by potentially maintaining modulus while recovering some pore fraction.

